{"id": "6074", "revid": "2164467", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6074", "title": "Carnforth", "text": "Carnforth is a small market town in Lancashire in Northwest England. It's best known for its railway station, location for the 1945 David Lean film \"Brief Encounter\", with the station clock ticking relentlessly to trammel the characters' lives. However the main reason to visit and stay here is to explore the nearby Arnside and Silverdale Area of Outstanding Natural Beauty, which straddles the border with Cumbria. \nGet in.\nBy train.\nThe London to Glasgow trains hurtle past, bypassing the station. Carnforth is served by two branch lines: west to Barrow-in-Furness (hourly, 50 min, via Silverdale, Arnside, Grange-over-Sands, and stations in Cartmel and Furness) and east to Leeds (every couple of hours, 1 hr 40 min along a scenic route via the Pennines and Keighley). Southbound, both branches join the main line to Lancaster (10 mins) then continue to Morecambe or to Preston. In the film, Noel Coward voices the station announcements for these destinations. is central in town. \nBy road.\nCarnforth is close to Junction 35 of M6, which runs north to Penrith, Carlisle and Scotland, and south to Preston and the Midlands.\nStagecoach Bus 555 runs from Lancaster hourly, daily, 35 min to Carnforth via Skerton, Hest Bank and Bolton-le-Sands. It continues north via Pine Lakes, Tewitfield, Milnthorpe, Kendal and Windermere, where it connects with the bus to Bowness, Ambleside, Grasmere and Keswick.\nA variant route 3 or 4 times a day is Stagecoach Bus 755, which runs from Bowness via Windermere and Kendal to Carnforth as above, then branches off to Morecambe and Heysham. This is the only direct bus between Carnforth and nearby Morecambe: otherwise change at Lancaster or take the train.\nBy bike.\nCarnforth is on National Cycle Route 6. From Lancaster this follows the canal north to Carnforth. To the north it follows the lanes through Warton, Yealand Conyers and Redmayne, Beetham, Milnthorpe and Hincaster to Kendal, via gentle terrain.\nGet around.\nThe trains to Barrow are good for exploring around Arnside and Silverdale, as you can take a bike on the train or hike between stations.\nBus 51 runs every couple of hours M-Sa via Warton, Yealand Conyers/Redmayne and Silverdale railway station to Silverdale village.\nTewitfield is on the hourly bus route between Carnforth and Kendal, so you can ride one way and walk the other along the canal.\nConnect.\nAs of April 2022, Carnforth has 4G from all UK carriers, but coverage is patchy from Three. 5G has not reached this area."}
