{"id": "6179", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6179", "title": "Cassadaga (Florida)", "text": "Cassadaga is a small unincorporated community in Western Volusia County, between Deltona and Lake Helen. \"Cassadaga\" is a Seneca Indian word meaning \"water beneath the rocks.\"\nUnderstand.\nCassadaga is best known for having a large number of psychics and \"Spiritualist Mediums\", and is sometimes referred to as being the \"Psychic Center of the World\".\nIts founder, George Colby, was led through the wilderness by three spirit guides to what is now the site of the Cassadaga Spiritualist Camp. On that site, the guides instructed him to establish a Spiritualist Camp, which was completed in 1894. It is a place like no other, where, Spiritualist Mediums and Spiritualist Healers can live in peace without interference from the world outside their gates.\nThe village sits way off Interstate 4 (you won't find it unless you're looking for it) between Orlando and Daytona Beach. \nThe town is open for visitors, and attracts many thousands a year passing through.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe only way to reach Cassadaga is by car. From I-4, take Exit-114. Turn onto Highway 472 toward Orange City/Deland. At the traffic light, turn right onto Dr. Martin Luther King Parkway. Turn right at the first right onto Cassadaga Road (County Route 4139). Continue 1.5 mi to the intersection with Stevens Street. The Camp Bookstore and Information Center is on the right.\nSee.\nParks include,, and.\nDo.\nOne of the main reasons to visit Cassadaga Spiritual Camp, is to have a Spiritualist reading by a camp-certified medium. This is a part of their religion and they take it very seriously.\nSleep.\nThe Spanish Mission-style Cassadaga Hotel, which is not an official part of the camp, dominates the landscape at the camp entrance.\nStay safe.\nDefinitely do not sleep in your car."}
{"id": "6182", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=6182", "title": "Casselberry", "text": "Casselberry is a city in Seminole County, Greater Orlando, Florida.\nUnderstand.\nCasselberry is one of many suburbs on the northeastern side of Orlando. It is between two important suburbs of the city: Sanford to the north, which is the location of the Sanford/Orlando Airport and a lakefront, and Winter Park to the south, the location of Rollins College.\nLongwood is a suburban community near Casselberry. Like Casselberry, it is primarily residential and those traveling to Longwood will want to stay in one of the larger local suburbs. However Casselberry's few restaurants and parks are within a few miles of Longwood and there is little to separate these two suburbs.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nUS-17/92 is the main route through the Longwood/Casselberry area. It connects the towns to the more central parts of the Orlando metro area to the immediate south. I-4 goes just west of the town.\nBy train.\nThere are two Sunrail stations in Casselberry's vicinity, one being for Longwood and the other being for Altamonte Springs. Neither are in great locations for visiting Casselberry itself, though, so you'll need to have a car or know someone with a car to connect from either of these stations.\nSleep.\nFor a wider selection of hotels, there a number of suburbs in Greater Orlando that provide options. You can try Maitland/Winter Park to the south."}
